Stage 3 — Gmail OAuth and local connector

The next goal was to test one real Gmail account rather than an organisation-
wide deployment.

The Google Cloud setup involved:

enabling the Gmail API;

configuring the OAuth consent screen in testing mode;

adding the test Gmail account as an OAuth test user;

creating an OAuth Desktop client;

downloading credentials.json; and

completing the browser authorization flow to create token.json.

The OAuth scope used by the scanner is:

https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly

This permits reading but not sending, deleting or modifying email. When the
original sole-owner/test account was suspended, the OAuth project and
credentials had to be replaced and the account authorized again.

A local FastAPI service was introduced because the published frontend could not
directly store Gmail secrets. The backend runs with:

uvicorn api:app --reload

It exposes:

Endpoint

Purpose

GET /

Health, backend version, model state and capabilities

GET /api/messages

Gmail Inbox metadata and saved scan status

GET /api/messages/{message_id}/analysis

Cached or fresh analysis of one message

CORS and private-network headers allow the browser interface to communicate
with the connector on 127.0.0.1:8000 while OAuth credentials remain on the
computer.

Stage 4 — Live Gmail message analysis

The backend retrieves Gmail message metadata for the Inbox and retrieves the
full selected message only when analysis is requested. It parses raw Gmail MIME
structures, including nested parts, plain text, HTML, headers and attachment
names.

The initial live analyser contained 12 rule-based checks. Machine learning was
later added as the thirteenth check.

The current 13 checks are:

SPF Verification

DKIM Verification

DMARC Alignment

Header Address Consistency

Sender Route & IP

Sender Domain Safety

Machine-Learning Classification

URL & Redirect Indicators

Urgency & Pressure Language

Sensitive-Data Request

Attachment Type Safety

Hidden HTML Content

Message-ID Structure

The system extracts and displays evidence but deliberately does not:

open links;

follow redirects;

download attachment bodies;

execute files; or

render untrusted HTML.

Stage 5 — Machine-learning model

A text-classification model was trained using public phishing and legitimate
email data derived from the Nazario and Enron corpora. The saved model is:

models/phishing_model.joblib

The model evaluates the combined subject and plain-text body and returns:

predicted class;

phishing probability;

confidence; and

risk band.

The earlier internal train/test result was approximately 98.88%, but this
was treated only as an internal figure because related corpus data could appear
in both model development and later demonstrations.

