Stage 8 — Controlled live-email testing

Legitimate and simulated phishing messages were sent between test Gmail
accounts. Tests used only reserved example.com URLs and no real malware.

An early comparison produced:

legitimate mail: 0/100, Low risk;

phishing-style mail: 37/100, Suspicious.

The phishing sample remained below Malicious because it originated from a real
Gmail account, so SPF, DKIM and DMARC passed, and a defanged hxxps link was not
recognized as a normal URL. This became an important demonstration point:

An authenticated sender can still send malicious content. Authentication
validates infrastructure and domain alignment; it does not prove benign
intent.

A reusable safe-email pack was created:

gmail_phishing_test_pack.csv

It includes credential theft, executive fraud, gift cards, payroll, MFA/OTP,
invoice, KYC, delivery, HR and vendor-payment scenarios, plus legitimate
controls.

An automated Gmail sender was also created:

send_phishing_tests.py

It uses the gmail.send OAuth scope, a separate sender_token.json, reserved
example.com URLs and no attachments. It was restricted to the controlled test
recipient in the project version. This utility is for authorized testing only.

Stage 9 — Offline Jose Nazario MBOX analysis

The Jose Nazario Phishing Corpus was added as an offline pipeline stress test.
It was not used as independent accuracy evidence because Nazario-derived data
had already contributed to model training.

The adapter is:

analyze_mbox.py

Its workflow is:

open the raw MBOX without rendering it;

parse headers and MIME parts;

extract text, HTML structure and attachment names;

convert the message into the Gmail-like dictionary accepted by
analyse_message();

run the same 13-check pipeline; and

save CSV summary and JSONL detail files.

The outputs are:

offline_results/nazario/nazario_analysis_summary.csv
offline_results/nazario/nazario_analysis_details.jsonl

