Stage 11 — Persistent scan history: backend v2.3

The next problem was that every message returned to Pending after an Inbox
refresh or backend restart. This made a mailbox with dozens of messages
impractical because the user appeared to need to analyse everything again.

Backend v2.3 introduced:

scan_history.db

This SQLite database stores:

Gmail message ID;

scan timestamp;

score;

verdict;

severity; and

complete analysis JSON.

The Gmail message ID is the persistent key. The behavior became:

unseen messages: PENDING;

previously analysed messages: saved score and verdict;

View saved analysis: return cached evidence without rescanning;

Run again: call the analysis endpoint with force=true and replace the
saved result.

Messages analysed before installing v2.3 required one final scan because no
historical database record existed for them.

Stage 12 — Complete-Inbox pagination: backend v2.4

The v2.3 endpoint still contained:

maxResults=20

Therefore the interface could never show more than the newest 20 Inbox
messages. The frontend itself could render more, so the limitation was entirely
inside api.py.

Backend v2.4 changed /api/messages to:

request up to 500 Gmail references per page;

follow every nextPageToken until Gmail returns no further page;

collect every message carrying the INBOX label;

fetch its display metadata; and

attach any saved SQLite scan summary.

The history lookup was also divided into 500-ID batches so a large Inbox would
not exceed SQLite's bound-parameter limit.

The health endpoint now reports:

{
  "version": "2.4",
  "scan_history": "persistent",
  "inbox_loading": "all_messages"
}

During installation, Windows repeatedly loaded an older local api.py. A
uniquely named api_v24.py was temporarily provided and started with:

uvicorn api_v24:app --reload

This bypassed the stale file. In the final VS Code package, the canonical file
is once again backend/api.py, and it contains v2.4.

