7. Main risks and safeguards

Gmail privacy

The read-only token can still expose sensitive mailbox content. Use a dedicated
test account and revoke access when testing is complete.

Local API exposure

The FastAPI service has no user authentication. It must remain on
127.0.0.1; it should not be exposed with --host 0.0.0.0, a public tunnel or
an open proxy.

False positives and false negatives

The score supports analyst triage; it is not proof. No automated blocking,
credential reset or disciplinary action should be based solely on the prototype
verdict.

Dangerous datasets

Historical corpora can contain active links or malware. Do not click links,
render HTML, extract files or forward corpus messages through Gmail.

Data retention

scan_history.db can contain message-derived evidence. Protect it, define a
retention period and delete it when no longer needed.

Test-email reputation

Sending large volumes of phishing-like content may trigger provider anti-abuse
systems. Use small authorized batches only between accounts under the team's
control.

8. Accurate hackathon positioning

A defensible description is:

PhishGuard is an explainable phishing-analysis and DFIR prototype that
combines live read-only Gmail ingestion, 12 forensic/content rules, a text
classifier, persistent scan history and offline MBOX processing. It reached
86% accuracy on a separate controlled 50-message validation set and flagged
90.37% of the analysable Nazario corpus during pipeline stress testing. The
Nazario figure is detection coverage, not independent accuracy.

The key demonstration message is that email authentication alone is not enough:
SPF, DKIM and DMARC may all pass for a compromised or malicious authenticated
sender, while content, request context, URLs and ML signals still indicate risk.

9. Recommended next improvements

Add safe URL canonicalization, redirect tracing and domain-age/reputation
enrichment.

Add brand/lookalike detection using edit distance and a protected brand list.

Add an isolated attachment-download service with hashes, magic bytes and
antivirus/YARA scanning.

Connect a real CAPE or Cuckoo sandbox through a separate restricted worker.

Introduce authenticated users, RBAC, encryption and audit logs.

Load very large mailboxes incrementally while preserving complete search.

Validate on recent, licensed, independently held-out phishing campaigns.

Add analyst disposition and feedback for controlled model improvement.

Current state: PhishGuard v2.4 is operational for controlled local Gmail
testing, complete-Inbox viewing, explainable message analysis, saved scan
history and offline MBOX batch analysis. It is a hackathon prototype and not a
production email-security gateway.
